Z||000009||olympic||11-15-1999||09:16 PM||||Your right unless you live here in Canada where we used the imperial gallon(4.5 litres)before going metric. The US gallon is 3.8 litres. Anyways, gas just went up 5.4 cents per litre in my town today to 63.9 cents/litre and there are rumors it could go to 85 cents or higher this winter.I run a small cab company here and that could just about break me.<BR>By the way, you've all seen the stickers in the windows of new cars that list the options and estimate the cars miles per gallon right? Guess what they use to rate a cars efficiency in Canada...kilometeres/litre? nah, to simple...try litres per 100 km. What a joke.<BR>   Here's a interesting fact I read on the net a while back. The retail cost of a litre of gas is almost the same throuought the world at about 32 cents US per litre. The big price differences are caused by taxes. Here in Saskatchewan, taxes make up more than half the cost of gas. Taxes also make up about 75% of the price of booze($15 a dozen) and cigarettes($6.50 a pack)!!! ||||reg||13
Z||000010||theogre||11-15-1999||11:12 PM||||Most readers here in the U.S. wouldn't believe what portion of gas prices is tax. Especially in many cities which add municipal taxes to everything. They've all been taught that it's the oil companies fault. I worked in a gas station once upon a time so I've seen the numbers.<P>It's been a long time since I checked, but last I looked, it was around half the pump price. In some citys/states it's more than half the pump price. I doubt it's changed much, as raw petrol prices have climbed so to have the taxes.<P>Think about that the next time your city, the state, or the feds, want to add another nickle or more.<P>BTW...... check your state but if you are buying pump Diesle for heating use there is often a discount on the taxes. Heating buyers don't pay some of the "road tax" in many areas. Check at "truck stops" they usually know more about this than the local gas & go. (I used to heat with pump diesle when I lived in Syracuse,NY. Worked better than the Kero, and burned cleaner. The pump diesle has aditives in it that also keep your furnace cleaner.)<P>Cliff, I don't know what the regs are there, but have you considered running a grain alcohol mix? EFI motors can often tolerate it quite well. It's not as rough on things as methanol. I'd like to try it in mine just to see how it runs but I don't have a cheap source. At that price for gas the ethynol has got to be cheaper. If I remember right you might could ever reduce the octain number for the gas with the alcohol added. I seem to recall knocking is reduced by adding it.||||reg||1
Z||000011||87GT||11-15-1999||11:34 PM||||Florida has quite a few add on taxes when it comes to gas, cigs, beer or whatever else.  The reason why in my opinion, is becuase there is not a state income tax in Florida.  They get their money through bloated taxes on items.  93 octane in Florida, I believe is around $1.51 right now.(gallon)||||reg||1
