Z||000018||Cliff Pennock||11-16-1999||04:35 AM||||RFMike: 95 octane is the lowest we can get here.<P>Olympic: Yes, a litre of gas is US$0.32 here too. The rest is tax. It all started back in the seventies when there supposedly was an oil-shortage. We were not allowed to drive a car on sundays and the government raised the price of a litre of gas with US$0.15 temporarily. Not long thereafter it was raised with another 15 cents, again temporarily. The government found out that no matter how expensive they made gasoline, people would still drive their cars so they raised it again and again and never lowered it. On top of that we pay road-tax of about $400 per year. Next year, if you want to drive your car in certain cities between certain hours, you'll have to pay US$2.50 per day extra.<P>Taxes here in Holland are incredibly high - amongst the highest in the world - and the Dutch government taxes <B>everything</B>. Some professor once calculated that for every dollar we make, about 90-95 cents go to the government through taxes.||||reg||1
Z||000019||ggarsk||11-16-1999||05:24 AM||||Wow,and I thought the 30-40% in taxes here was bad. It's around $1.40 for 93oct here in Oregon. Of that 43 cents is taxes. I don't think I will ever complain about the price of gas again.||||reg||8
