Z||000031||batboy||11-18-1999||11:13 PM||||Ok, how many of you fellow old timers remember the "gas wars" of the mid 1960's? I was just a kid in Iowa then, but I remember all the gas stations in town dropping their prices one summer in order to draw in more customers in what was called a gas war. The lowest I can recall was 25 cents per gallon. Of course, these were all "full service" stations then. That was back when the attendant pumped your gas, washed the windshield, and checked the oil for you.||||reg||1
Z||000032||theogre||11-18-1999||11:26 PM||||Olympic,<P>Yes, gas does change over time. There is some debate over how much change in how much time. Even so it's one of the reasons I try to stay with stations that do high volumes. such stations get delivery one or more times a week.<P>If you get a station that is reasonably good at managing it's tanks then none of the gas should be getting very old. Gas stations, even slow ones, avoid buying more gas than they think they will use in a week or 2.<P>One reason is that they don't want to spend the money on gas they can't move.<P>Another is that they don't want to get caught with full tanks if the price drops. They don't want to be stuck with fuel that cost them more last week than it does this week. It means they can't follow their competitors price drops untill the more expensive stuff is run down. Such s situation could cost them customers.||||reg||1
Z||000033||Phil||11-19-1999||06:10 AM||||I think the cheapest gas I ever bought was in a gas war around '63. I was driving through Alabama and Gulftane was $ 0.12/gal and a McDonalds was $0.15||||reg||1
