Z||000035||olympic||11-19-1999||01:55 PM||||My parents showed me some receipts from the late fifties. Gas was 16 cents per gallon. My dad made $200 per month as a mechanic and with it he made a car payment, paid rent and raised a family. I saw a program on TV a while back that showed how the price of goods has been doubling roughly every ten years since 1940. For example, a car that cost $500 in 1940 cost $1000 in 1950, $2000 in 1960 and so on til $32000 in 2000. Can you imagine paying $64000 for an average car in 2010!!!!  ||||reg||1
Z||000036||DJRice||11-19-1999||04:07 PM||||Yeah, I can see that, especially with my measily little cost of living wage increases.<BR>The cost of everything will continue to double, and pay rates will continue to be raised by less than 3% per year.<P>Thats fair.  ||||reg||1
Z||000037||RFMike||11-19-1999||07:55 PM||||My dad was a machinist, and in the early sixties, he was in the process of raising 12 kids, making a car payment, a house payment, owned a boat, and we always had food on the table.  There's no way a machinist could do that today.<BR>Of course, my brothers and I all hunted and fished, and filled the freezer with meat.  In the fall, my folks would buy 800 pounds of potatoes at a potato farm for $2.50 per 100# burlap sack.  We stored them in a root cellar, and finished them off by spring.  We bought beef on the hoof from my uncle, and butchered it ourselves.  Same thing with pork from another uncle.  We had a huge vegetable garden that I spent many adolescent Saturdays weeding.  That's why I hate gardening.<BR>Just about nobody lives this way anymore, and the country is probably worse off for it.<BR>Sorry for the off topic ramble, but I kind of think Fiero owners have some of that mindset.<BR>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000038||RFMike||11-19-1999||09:31 PM||||Doing whatever it takes to survive and flourish, that is.||||reg||1
